---
import fs from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PhotoSlot from "../../components/PhotoSlot.astro";
import VideoSlot from "../../components/VideoSlot.astro";

const exists = (p: string) => fs.existsSync(path.join(process.cwd(), "public", p));
const heroExists = exists("images/safe.webp");
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="The Jentosy Project is a 501(c)(3) nonprofit advancing kidney health equity for underserved communities, starting in the Bronx."
>
  <!-- HERO — image only, no text on the photo. A light, flat tint (not a
       fading gradient) keeps it feeling of a piece with the site without
       the photo washing out at the bottom. Crisp, deliberate cut into
       the article below — no blend. -->
  <section class="on-dark about-hero" data-parallax-section>
    {heroExists ? (
      <img class="about-hero-bg" src="/images/safe.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" data-parallax-bg />
    ) : (
      <div class="about-hero-placeholder">
        <p class="about-hero-placeholder-note">
          Hero photo placeholder — drop <code>safe.webp</code> into <code>public/images/</code>{" "}
          and it'll appear here automatically.
        </p>
      </div>
    )}
    <div class="about-hero-tint"></div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE ARTICLE — one continuous piece, not a stack of sections. ABOUT
       is the page's real h1 — pulled up over the hero via a visual-only
       shift (position, not margin) so the text below it never moves. -->
  <article class="on-light about-article">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h1 class="about-title reveal">About</h1>

      <p class="reveal" style="--reveal-delay:0.1s">
        Kidney disease rarely announces itself. It can move through the body for years without a
        single symptom — by the time most people notice, a third of their kidney function may
        already be gone.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal" style="--reveal-delay:0.14s">
        What determines whether someone catches it in time has less to do with biology than
        geography. Where you live shapes what you know about your risk long before it shapes what
        care you can get — a zip code can predict whether you ever hear the words{" "}
        <em>family history of kidney disease</em> at all.
      </p>

      <p class="pull reveal">
        This isn't an accident of nature. It's poverty, schools that never taught kidney health,
        neighborhoods without a nephrologist nearby, generations who learned not to trust a system
        that hadn't earned it.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">
        Kidney disease doesn't discriminate on its own. The systems around it do.
      </p>

      <!-- THE ORGANIZATION -->
      <p class="eyebrow chapter reveal">The Organization</p>
      <h2 class="article-heading reveal">Built to close that gap.</h2>

      <p class="reveal">
        The Jentosy Project exists to change what people know before it's too late to act on it.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        It's an education-focused nonprofit — not a research lab, a treatment center, or a
        fundraising engine for someone else's cure. Its job is narrower, and more urgent: making
        sure people in underserved communities understand kidney disease early enough for it to
        matter.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Education sounds modest next to <em>cure</em> or <em>transplant</em>. It isn't. Chronic
        kidney disease is usually treatable if caught early — the failure is rarely medical, it's
        informational. People don't get tested because no one told them they were at risk. That's
        the gap this organization exists to close; the specifics live on the{" "}
        <a href="/about/what-we-do/">What We Do</a> page.
      </p>

      <!-- WHY THE BRONX -->
      <p class="eyebrow chapter reveal">Why the Bronx</p>
      <h2 class="article-heading reveal">A starting point, not an endpoint.</h2>

      <p class="reveal">
        The work begins in the Bronx — not because it's exceptional, but because it's
        representative.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        The borough carries some of the starkest health disparities in the country: elevated
        diabetes and hypertension, the leading drivers of kidney failure; a population
        disproportionately affected by APOL1 gene variants; neighborhoods where the nearest
        nephrologist might as well be in another state. Nearly every one of those facts has a twin
        elsewhere — Detroit, rural Mississippi, South Los Angeles.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Starting here is a decision about where to prove the model works, not where the problem
        ends. What's built — the trust, the relationships with clinics and community groups — is
        meant to travel.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="article-image reveal">
      <PhotoSlot src="/images/NtX0s.webp" alt="A New York City subway sign reading Uptown & The Bronx Express, with the 4 and 5 line bullets" ratio="3 / 2" />
      <figcaption>A community, not a case study.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="container narrow">
      <!-- THE NAME -->
      <p class="eyebrow chapter reveal">The Name</p>
      <h2 class="article-heading reveal">Two women. One chain. A debt worth a lifetime.</h2>

      <p class="reveal">
        The organization's name is not an acronym, and it isn't a place. It's two people.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        In 2009, Robert Sanchez — who would go on to found The Jentosy Project — received his
        first kidney transplant, a living donation from a friend. FSGS, the disease that had
        already destroyed his own kidneys, found its way into the new one. By 2014 he was back on
        dialysis, three times a week.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        In 2019, out of options, he posted on Facebook asking if anyone would donate a kidney.
        Within a week, organ matchmaker Chaya Lipschutz found a match: Elizabeth Antosy.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Then, the night before surgery, his transplant surgeon called with a diagnosis of kidney
        cancer, found too late to operate. The transplant was off.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Most people would have walked away. Elizabeth didn't — she joined a kidney exchange chain
        instead, matching willing but incompatible donors with strangers across a linked series of
        transplants. Once Robert cleared cancer, that chain matched him with a new donor: Jen
        Keller. On October 20, 2021, the two were transplanted. Elizabeth's decision to stay in
        the chain ultimately gave <strong>eight people</strong> new kidneys.
      </p>

      <p class="pull reveal">The organization is named for both of them — Jen, and An-tosy.</p>

      <figure class="story-video-figure reveal">
        <VideoSlot
          src="/videos/where-the-name-comes-from.mp4"
          youtubeId="nsQ35ZgiWOk"
          ratio="16 / 9"
          title="Bronx man finally getting kidney transplant after years of waiting — CBS News New York"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Robert on Jen, Elizabeth, and the chain that connected them. Independently covered by{" "}
          <a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/newyork/news/kidney-transplant-kidney-chain-robert-sanchez-elizabeth-antosy/">CBS News New York</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="reveal">
        Robert tells this story in far more detail than fits here, because the specifics
        matter — not just that two women helped him, but who they were and what it cost them.
        Read the extended version on the <a href="/about/team/">Meet The Team</a> page.
      </p>

      <!-- STORYTELLING AS MEDICINE -->
      <p class="eyebrow chapter reveal">Storytelling as Medicine</p>
      <h2 class="article-heading reveal">Data tells you where. Stories tell you why.</h2>

      <p class="reveal">
        Ask Robert why the organization leads with stories instead of statistics, and he'll tell
        you: the statistics never explained anything to him.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Data tells you where a problem exists. It rarely tells you why. A chart showing that Black
        Americans develop kidney failure at four times the rate of white Americans is true and
        important — and almost useless to someone trying to understand what it means for their
        own body. What changed things for Robert wasn't a statistic. It was hearing from someone
        who had already lived through it.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        That's the premise the organization is built on: storytelling isn't an add-on to health
        education. It is the work.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Stories reduce fear by replacing the unknown with the specific. They build trust in a
        medical system that hasn't always earned it, because a shared story carries a credibility
        no pamphlet can. They turn a diagnosis into something survivable, because someone else
        already survived it. And they remind the person hearing them that they're more than a
        diagnosis.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="article-image article-image-inset reveal">
      <PhotoSlot src="/images/healingheart.webp" alt="A heart formed from interlocking human silhouettes, in warm earth tones" ratio="1 / 1" />
      <figcaption>Lived experience, treated as evidence.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="reveal">
        Patients who hear from peers with similar diagnoses report lower anxiety, more confidence
        in their treatment decisions, and stronger relationships with their care teams. For
        communities carrying generations of reasonable mistrust, a story does what an outreach
        campaign can't — it isn't asking to be believed. It just is.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        The Jentosy Project treats lived experience as a form of evidence, alongside clinical
        data, not instead of it. Robert's story — and the stories that will eventually join
        his — aren't testimonials bolted onto the mission. They are the mission, in practice.
      </p>

      <!-- MISSION + VISION, woven in -->
      <p class="reveal">
        All of this adds up to something the organization states plainly: The Jentosy Project
        advances health equity for underserved communities by educating people about rare kidney
        diseases — including FSGS and APOL1-mediated kidney disease — and building the
        partnerships needed to close the gap in kidney care. It isn't complicated. It doesn't need
        to be.
      </p>

      <p class="pull reveal">
        The vision is just as direct: a future where no one's zip code determines their access to
        kidney health education, early detection, or life-saving care.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">
        Getting there starts with education — the one part of this equation that doesn't require
        insurance, a referral, or proximity to a research hospital. Just someone willing to
        explain, and someone willing to listen.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="article-image article-image-inset reveal">
      <PhotoSlot src="/images/treeoflife.webp" alt="Cupped hands cradling a tree that grows from a pair of kidneys" ratio="5 / 4" />
      <figcaption>Rooted in care, growing toward equity.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="container narrow">
      <!-- WHY EDUCATION COMES FIRST -->
      <p class="eyebrow chapter reveal">Why Education Comes First</p>
      <h2 class="article-heading reveal">Prevention only works before the crisis.</h2>

      <p class="reveal">
        It would be easier to build an organization around transplantation — it's the part of
        Robert's story with the clearest arc: crisis, donor, surgery, recovery. But by the time
        someone needs a transplant, the moment education could have changed the outcome has
        usually passed.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Most kidney disease doesn't announce a deadline. It gives warnings, if anyone's paying
        attention: protein in the urine, blood pressure that won't come down, family members who
        died young of something no one ever named. Catching those warnings takes knowing what to
        look for, and somewhere to bring the question.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Health literacy is the first. Someone who understands what FSGS is, or what carrying two
        high-risk APOL1 variants means, can ask the right question at the right appointment —
        instead of learning the term for the first time in a hospital bed. Community education,
        delivered in plain language by people already trusted, spreads that understanding faster
        than any pamphlet.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Trust is the harder one. Decades of documented harm have given communities good reason to
        be cautious. That doesn't dissolve because a nonprofit shows up with good intentions — it
        dissolves slowly, through showing up more than once, partnering with organizations already
        trusted, and telling the truth even when it's complicated.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        None of this replaces better access to nephrologists, fairer transplant waitlists, or
        research that makes FSGS more treatable. Transplantation equity remains part of where this
        is headed. But prevention only works before the crisis — which is why education comes
        first, not instead.
      </p>

      <!-- LOOKING FORWARD -->
      <p class="eyebrow chapter reveal">Looking Forward</p>
      <h2 class="article-heading reveal">A future geography can't predict.</h2>

      <p class="reveal">
        Picture a country where a kidney disease diagnosis doesn't depend on which zip code
        delivered it.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        Where a teenager in the Bronx and a teenager in rural Georgia have the same chance of
        learning, early, what their family history means. Where no one hears the word{" "}
        <em>nephrologist</em> for the first time in an emergency room. Where every patient's story
        becomes part of how the next person understands their own — and survives it a little more
        easily because of what came before.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        That future doesn't require a miracle. It requires what changed Robert's life: someone
        willing to explain, someone willing to listen, and the belief that where you live should
        never be the reason you didn't know in time.
      </p>

      <p class="pull reveal article-closer">It starts here. It doesn't end here.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="on-dark section-tight cta-band">
    <div class="container cta-inner reveal">
      <h2 class="h2">Help advance health equity in kidney care.</h2>
      <div class="cluster gap-3" style="margin-top:var(--space-5);">
        <a href="/partner-with-us/" class="btn btn-steel-outline">Partner With Us</a>
        <a href="/donate/" class="btn btn-rust">Donate</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .narrow {
    max-width: 700px;
  }

  /* ---------- Hero (photo only) ---------- */

  .about-hero {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 58vh;
    overflow: clip;
  }

  .about-hero-bg {
    position: absolute;
    inset: -16% 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 132%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 30%;
    will-change: transform;
  }

  /* Flat tint, not a fade — the photo reads clearly right to its bottom
     edge, and the cut into the article below is deliberate, not hazy. */
  .about-hero-tint {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: rgba(29, 42, 30, 0.22);
  }

  .about-hero-placeholder {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: var(--space-6);
  }

  .about-hero-placeholder-note {
    max-width: 46ch;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--ink-fg-muted);
  }

  .about-hero-placeholder-note code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11.5px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  /* ---------- The article ---------- */

  .about-article {
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: clamp(var(--space-8), 8vw, var(--space-9));
  }

  /* Same body type as the rest of the site — no page-specific font-size,
     just the shared default — with only the paragraph spacing tightened. */
  .about-article .narrow p {
    color: var(--fg-soft);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
  }

  /* The page's real h1. Shifted up with position (not margin), so its
     own box still reserves the same space in the flow — everything
     below it stays exactly where it was. Tighter tracking and the
     heaviest weight Cormorant Garamond actually loads (700; 800 isn't
     a loaded weight and was silently falling back) for real boldness
     instead of a synthetic fallback. */
  .about-title {
    position: relative;
    top: -2rem;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(72px, 13vw, 180px);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .about-title {
      top: 0;
    }
  }

  /* Chapter labels reuse the site's shared .eyebrow class as-is; this
     just adds the extra breathing room before each new chapter starts. */
  .eyebrow.chapter {
    margin-top: clamp(var(--space-7), 7vw, var(--space-8));
  }

  /* Cormorant Garamond italic only loads at 400/500 — 600 was falling
     back the same way the old title weight was. */
  .article-heading {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: clamp(28px, 4.2vw, 44px);
    line-height: 1.18;
    color: var(--fg);
    max-width: 18ch;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
  }

  .about-article .pull {
    margin: var(--space-5) 0;
  }

  .article-closer {
    font-size: clamp(22px, 2.6vw, 28px);
    border-left-color: var(--steel);
  }

  /* ---------- Article images ---------- */

  .article-image {
    margin: clamp(var(--space-7), 6vw, var(--space-8)) auto;
    max-width: var(--content-max);
    padding-inline: clamp(20px, 5vw, 56px);
  }

  .article-image-inset {
    max-width: 460px;
  }

  .article-image figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }

  /* ---------- Where the name comes from (video figure) ---------- */

  .story-video-figure {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
  }

  .story-video-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }

  .cta-inner {
    text-align: center;
    max-width: 640px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { gsap } from "gsap";
  import { ScrollTrigger } from "gsap/ScrollTrigger";

  gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);

  const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
  const section = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-parallax-section]");
  const bg = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-parallax-bg]");

  // Gentle big-story parallax on the hero photo only — the image is sized
  // larger than its box (see .about-hero-bg) so the drift never reveals
  // empty space at the edges.
  if (section && bg && !prefersReducedMotion) {
    gsap.to(bg, {
      yPercent: 12,
      ease: "none",
      scrollTrigger: {
        trigger: section,
        start: "top top",
        end: "bottom top",
        scrub: true,
      },
    });
  }
</script>
